<blockquote>
<p>SCP-2889 is also used as a tourist attraction in the months before the yearly harvest.</p>
</blockquote>
<p>Shouldn't that be past-tense, since the town has been contained?</p>
<blockquote>
<p>When input into a search engine, the phrase &quot;that sweet, clean, Freshwater smell&quot; results in a link to the tourism page of Freshwater, VT.</p>
</blockquote>
<p>Is this part of the anomaly? If not, why hasn't the Foundation shut down the tourism site yet?</p>
<blockquote>
<p>Interviews with inhabitants of Freshwater show that they are aware of both the effects of SCP-2889</p>
</blockquote>
<p>Shouldn't that be &quot;the effects of SCP-2889 oil&quot;?</p>
<blockquote>
<p>Unidentified: I know, love. You're hungry. I know. But look! Right on time. The lure works, love. It always does.</p>
</blockquote>
<p>Shouldn't that be spoken in ancient Greek?</p>
<blockquote>
<p>The inhabitants of Freshwater use SCP-2889 for logging purposes, as well as harvesting and selling the essential oils exuded by SCP-2889.</p>
</blockquote>
<p>Might want to refer to the oils as SCP-2889-1 or SCP-2889-A.</p>
